01220703125, -0.00399017333984375, -0.026458740234375, -0.004650115966796875, 0.03424072265625, -0.007122039794921875, 0.00531005859375, 0.01068878173828125, 0.0059051513671875, -0.0125274658203125, 0.0187835693359375, 0.002834320068359375, -0.01213836669921875, -0.0005831718444824219, -0.0316162109375, -0.007061004638671875, -0.0208892822265625, -1.806020736694336e-05, 0.01751708984375, -0.0076904296875, -0.0018062591552734375, 0.00390625, 0.0035419464111328125, -0.004688262939453125, -0.00923919677734375, 0.01158905029296875, 0.052459716796875, -0.038177490234375, -0.006404876708984375, -0.01207733154296875, 0.0152740478515625, -0.00934600830078125, 0.004974365234375, 0.0188751220703125, -0.002536773681640625, 0.011016845703125, -0.038909912109375, -0.004840850830078125, -0.011688232421875, -0.018035888671875, 0.029937744140625, -0.023681640625, 0.0313720703125, -0.06298828125, -0.0173492431640625, -0.0218353271484375, -0.008758544921875, 0.0024814605712890625, -0.01137542724609375, -0.013031005859375, -0.007305145263671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work describes an extension of the permanental process model to include fixed effects, motivated by the need to account for known covariates or baseline trends in scientific data. Incorporating fixed effects allows for more flexible modeling and the integration of prior knowledge about systematic influences on the intensity function. We begin by placing a prior distribution on the fixed effects coefficients and consider the diffuse prior limit, where the prior becomes increasingly non-informative. In this limit, the representer theorem\u2014a result that characterizes solutions to certain regularized estimation problems\u2014shows that the intensity function of the permanental process naturally decomposes into a sum of a fixed effects term and a function belonging to a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS). This decomposition clarifies the contribution of fixed effects and nonparametric components.\n\nWe further demonstrate that the limiting equivalent kernel, which arises from the interaction between the prior and the model structure, defines an RKHS whose squared norm corresponds precisely to the regularization penalty imposed in the limiting case. Specifically, this penalty measures the complexity of the nonparametric component, and the equivalence is established by analyzing the form of the regularization term as the prior on the fixed effects becomes diffuse.\n\nThis framework enables straightforward scientific interpretation of permanental process models: fixed effects capture interpretable, known influences, while the RKHS component models residual structure. Domain knowledge can be easily incorporated by specifying relevant fixed effects or choosing kernels that reflect prior beliefs about the data. However, this extension assumes that the fixed effects are linearly related to the intensity function and that the prior on their coefficients can be made sufficiently diffuse; care should be taken when these assumptions do not hold."}}